00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:56.470
My fast fact number two, Sam, you might like this.

83
00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:03.450
I did do a little bit of a deep dive on the use of flashbacks and clip shows in film

84
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.030
and television.

85
00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.390
I do really like this.

86
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:05.810
Yay.

87
00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:10.870
I'm very curious because I had a screenwriter in college who had a theory about flashbacks.

88
00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:11.510
Go firSt.

89
00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.150
You go.

90
00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:12.750
You go.

91
00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.390
All right.

92
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:20.690
Well, according to Taste of Cinema, the best use of flashbacks in a movie belongs to Citizen

93
00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.250
Kane with Godfather Part II coming in second.

94
00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:30.670
But this article said that Citizen Kane introduced many techniques that were fairly new to the film

95
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:37.970
world at the time, like the use of depth of field and this fragmented storytelling that went

96
00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.470
back and forth between present and past, different time periods.

97
00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:47.790
The website Industrial Scripts said that if you are writing a film and you want to study

98
00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:52.530
great uses of flashbacks, they suggest studying the following filMs.

99
00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:56.770
Memento, Forrest Gump, or Titanic.

100
00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:00.110
Yeah, because Titanic's told basically through flashback.

101
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:00.790
Yes.

102
00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:01.270
Yeah.

103
00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:06.510
According to this site, all the stories of these films are told mostly from the perspective

104
00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:07.270
of flashback.

105
00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.970
It also noted films like Kill Bill, Old Boy, and Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind,

106
00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:14.430
which are some of my favorite movies.

107
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:14.850
Mm-hmm.

108
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.970
So perhaps I like a flashback movie.

109
00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:18.610
You do.

110
00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:19.650
I guess so.

111
00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.770
Our show, these are not flashbacks.

112
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:29.450
This is actually a clip show, which is something different, because these flashbacks are not

113
00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.870
part of overall storytelling, so it's a different category.

114
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:39.890
All of these flashbacks are set up as sort of Toby looks off in the distance recollecting,

115
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:40.390
right?

116
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:47.290
Yes, but they are not forwarding any new story through their flashback.

117
00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.250
So that's why it enters the column of clip show.

118
00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:57.950
Wikipedia says that a clip show is an episode of a TV series that consists primarily of excerpts

119
00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:59.390
from previous episodes.

120
00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:03.650
They are also known by their nickname, Cheaters.

121
00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:04.650
Okay.

122
00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:05.750
It's a cheater show.

