146
00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.150
Hmm.

147
00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:09.730
It's all about the money, guys.

148
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:10.610
Follow the money.

149
00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:11.890
It's all about the money.

150
00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:19.110
The first use of a clip show appears to be the serial movie Robinson Crusoe of Clipper

151
00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:23.190
Island from 1936 for Republic FilMs.

152
00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:26.670
So they were supposed to make 12 chapters of this story.

153
00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:31.830
Each one was about 30 minutes long, like the length of a TV show, but it played in theaters.

154
00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.770
But I guess there was some bad weather, and they had to shut down filming.

155
00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:43.250
So writer Barry Shipman was forced to create two extra chapters to help recoup the lost

156
00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.350
production costs.

157
00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:50.990
So now he had to do 14 chapters in less time with less budget.

158
00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:58.850
So to do this, he created what is considered to be the first recap, which was Chapter 11,

159
00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.390
Agents of Disaster.

160
00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:05.550
He wrote a few original scenes where the characters recount their adventures to date.

161
00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:10.870
And then they used stock footage from previous chapters instead of shooting new sequences.

162
00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:18.570
Well, Republic Films was so thrilled by this little cheat that the recap chapter became

163
00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.370
standard procedure moving forward.

164
00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:21.650
How about that?

165
00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.790
So I think maybe Barry Shipman started it all?

